ing)\n- 1.5 = k = 1\n- 0.5 = k = 0, but for own valid trials one of these is present: a measured-point graph without g or with g from two arbitrary points, or a traceable multi-point fit with g but no measured-point graph at all\n- 0 = none of these. Examples: 2x/t² quotients only, g from total fall time, 9.81 inserted, an unsupported assertion, analysis of foreign trials only, a maintained misconception.\nForeign trials and further unanalysed trials never lower k. If only the drawn fitted line is missing while the fit is documented separately, this is a form defect and k is unchanged. Calculator and spreadsheet graphs with visible points are equivalent.\n\nlaw-a / law-b (2.5 each)\nWhat counts as a law:\n- any mathematically equivalent law explicitly formulated for the series, in any notation and in any section;\n- a fit equation x = a + m·t² only if it is explicitly interpreted as that series' law;\n- not: a bare fit output or the coordinate convention in the setup.\nOn the intercept a: if a is consistent with zero, this supports x_0 = 0. A conspicuous a requires an investigation of possible causes (time zero, reference point, initial push). A justified open cause is also sufficient; a causal interpretation of an uncertain offset is not required.\nFacets, each on valid own trials of the series:\n- F1: data-based check of the initial conditions, e.g. from early points, the intercept or a fit, consistent with the release method. A push is not evidence of rest. A data-established v_0 ≠ 0, determined by a justified quadratic fit and carried into the law, is fully valid; x(0) = 0 still holds.\n- F2: axis direction, origin x(0) = 0 at the actual release point, t = 0 at release, and v_0 stated. A reference such as 'as in A' counts. A fit offset in differently referenced measurement coordinates counts only with an explained translation x = x_meas − x_R or t = t_meas − t_R.\n- F3: own g from own trials of this series; a non-graphical determination is enough, because P-GRAPH is graded separately.\nBands:\n- 2.5 = F1–F3 present\n- 1.5 = exactly one facet missing (e.g. the check is only carried over from A, one F2 item is missing, or 9.81 m/s² is used instead of an own g)\n- 0 = two or more facets missing, no law, non-quadratic form, or a maintained misconception\nA law for foreign trials only has a local maximum of 0.\n\nunc-a-var / unc-b-var (0.5 each, method share)\n- 0.5 = an actually computed variation of a time or position boundary (e.g. t ± 1 frame or an endpoint ± reading limit) with resulting g, on a trial of the series analysed by the learner, own or openly declared foreign\n- 0 = otherwise\n\nunc-a-own / unc-b-own (0.5 each)\n- 0.5 = order of uncertainty justified with own numbers, namely from time/time-zero reading, position reading and trial scatter of the valid own trials of the series\n- 0 = otherwise\nAn unsupported confidence interval or invented precision does not satisfy this; a computed variation still counts under unc-*-var. Quadrature or simple addition of contributions is acceptable as orientation.\n\ncmp-quant (0.5, method share)\n- 0.5 = difference of the learner's own A and B estimates compared quantitatively with the stated uncertainties, with a bounded statement on the common model\n- 0 = no comparison or a qualitative one only, a copied value, or values adjusted towards a desired result\n\ncmp-limit (0.5)\n- 0.5 = at least one model limit (initial push, time zero, parallax or drag) concretely justified with the learner's own analysed A or B trials, e.g. via their time-zero variation, a trial rejected there, or the scale position. One clearly supported limit is enough.\n- 0 = generic list only, limits only on foreign trials, or no limit\n\nMinor slips\nThis covers arithmetic, sign, unit, rounding and label errors where the reasoning is clearly correct.\n- Deduction: 0.5 once, in the row where the error first occurs. A row never falls below 0, and its band is unchanged.\n- Follow-on steps correctly continued with the wrong value are not penalised again.\n- Correct reasoning may be shown by other evidence: the approach, the graph, the other series, or a follow-on step.\n- Self-corrected errors cost nothing. The same repeated form defect counts once.\n- Four independent slips cost at most 2 points.\n\nMisconception\nA conceptual error is a misconception if either condition holds:\n(a) It is explicitly justified or held as a rule or interpretation and not withdrawn. A single such sentence suffices.\n(b) It is reproduced independently in A and B, with the correct version appearing nowhere. Merely reusing a wrong value is not repetition.\nExamples:\n- P-EXP: foreign or computed data presented as own; values adjusted towards a result\n- P-GRAPH: slope of the x-t² line equated with g; slope of an x-t line read as g\n- P-LAW: x(t) = −½gt² despite a downward-positive axis; a linear fall law; x_0 = drop height despite the origin at release; a push treated as evidence of rest\nRules:\n- A maintained misconception sets every row of the affected group to 0, method shares included. This applies even if it is held in one series only.\n- Only a recognisable withdrawal removes it, without deduction.\n- A single uncommented numerical or sign error with no stated rule is a slip if the other evidence is correct.\n- Mere gaps in checking or stating are missing facets, not misconceptions.\n- Other groups are unaffected.\n\nUnsupported assertions and isolated findings\nUnsupported assertions do not satisfy the facet concerned:\n- 'g = 9.8 from the graph' without a graph: no graph or fit evidence\n- 'v_0 = 0 because released': F1 missing\n- an uncertainty without derivation: unc-*-own = 0\n- 'own measurement' without a record: D not established\nAn isolated correct calculation or statement does not demonstrate a whole performance and counts only within the bands. Examples: g = 2m without a fit; x = ½·9.81·t² unrelated to the series.\n\nModel-appropriate tolerances\n- Any honestly derived g from